A law will enter into force in Finland on Tuesday, which restricts the procurement of substances suitable for the manufacture of explosives.
In the future, private individuals will have to apply to the National Police Board for permission to purchase or hold substances or mixtures that could be used as the so-called precursor of explosives.
Such substances include hydrogen peroxide, nitromethane, nitric acid, potassium chlorate, potassium perchlorate, sodium chlorate and sodium perchlorate, and mixtures containing them, if the specified concentration values are exceeded.
Hydrogen peroxide is found, for example, in cleaning and detergents, but concentrations do not usually exceed the limit values.
Nitromethane is used, for example, in acceleration.
However, the precursors acquired before the entry into force of the Act may be retained and used until 2 March 2016.
