Lidl Finland is part of the German Lidl Group.
The company has more than 4 200 employees in Finland.
Ovarian cancer is more likely to die in sparsely populated areas due to a late diagnosis and reduced access to treatment
Angelina Jolie and her brother James have released the video as a tribute to their mother, who died of ovarian cancer in 2007.
Women living in rural Australia have a higher risk of dying from ovarian cancer than those living in cities.
Researchers reviewed the medical records of more than 1100 Australian women diagnosed with ovarian cancer in 2005 and found that only 35 percent of women lived more than five years after the diagnosis.
Senior Researcher Susan Jordan QIMR of the Berghofer Medical Research Institute confirms that women living in sparsely populated and distantical areas were 20 percent more likely to die during the study than women living in urban areas.
SMALL STUDY: New medicines may slow down lung and ovarian cancer
Researchers went through women's health history for seven years.
According to doctor Susan Jordan, a woman's age at the time of the diagnosis, the type of ovarian cancer, previous diseases and socio-economic status also contributed to the possibility of survival.
In older women and when the cancer was more widespread at the time of the diagnosis, the chances of survival were lowest.
Women living in sparsely populated and remote areas were 20 percent more likely to die during the study than women living in urban areas.
The study did not try to find out where women living outside cities are less likely to cope with ovarian cancer, but Jordan considers the possible causes of delayed diagnosis and inconvenienced access to treatment.
“Gynecological oncology specialists treat this disease best, and their clinics are mostly located in major cities,” Jordan says.
Although remote treatments are becoming more common and the importance of distance is diminishing, the researcher also suggests increasing the mobility of physicians so that specialists can treat patients closer to their homes, as well as programs to support patients treated far from home.
According to Dr. Jordan's study, the long-term survival rate of women with ovarian cancer is poor, regardless of geographical location, which emphasizes the need for better treatment and better cancer prevention programs.
Funded by Rio Tinto Ride to Conquer Cancer, the study is published today in the Medical Journal of Australia.
Elisha Neave, a 33-year-old mother living in Gold Coast in March 2012, was diagnosed with malignant ovarian cancer.
The City of Rovaniemi wants to strengthen snowmobile parking operations in Friendship Park, which is located next to Hotel Pohjanhovi.
On Monday, the City Board decided that the town plan proposal for Friendship Park and its surroundings will be made publicly available at the service point Osviita.
Opinions on the plan proposal are requested from the Environmental Committee and the Lapland ELY Centre.
With the city plan change, the city wants to enable tourism-related snowmobiling activities to continue in the area.
It is planned to form a park area, which includes the area between the current Friendship Park and between it and the river.
According to the proposal, a maximum of 200 snowmobiles will be permanently available between 15 November and 30 April.
Refueling points may also be placed in a specified part of the area.
Maintenance traffic is also allowed in the new park area.
The City of Rovaniemi has rented the Friendship Park area for safari companies for snowmobile use during the winter.
The lease was last renewed in the spring of 2014 and the agreement is valid until spring 2018.
Snowmobile parking operations and the plan change have attracted even loud opposition.
Residents of neighbouring apartment buildings have been bummed by both sound and concern about emissions.
However, emissions from the area have been studied, and there have been no observations exceeding the guidelines.
The renewed equipment is also lower-emission than the old one.
The friendship park as a parking area is also supported by the fact that if the starting point were moved further away, the hotel's safaria customers would have to be transported to safari departure points by buses, which would have an estimated 15 departures per day.
According to the city, the area and the surrounding area and the surrounding area is also wide enough for snowmobiles, better than the places presented as replacement starting points, such as, for example, next to the boat pier on the east side of the Ounasjoki bridges.
In terms of its location, Friendship Park is also good because the activities may expand to the Ounaspaviljonki area, for example, in the future.
Consolidating parking operations is part of the city strategy approved by the Rovaniemi City Council, according to which the city's goal is, among other things, to create a good framework for business operations by reserving sufficiently high-quality areas in logistically and functionally inexpensive locations.
The Friendship Park has also been marked as the starting point for snowmobiling in the city centre's partial master plan, which has not yet acquired legal force.
The decline in owner-occupied dwellings is reflected in garden shops
The decrease in the number of homeowners, especially in a group of people under the age of 35, could lead to a reduction in the turnover of horticultural stores by tens of millions, as today’s young consumers reach the “main target group” for horticultural sales for their ages. The Financial Times reported on a study by the British Horticultural Trades Association (HTA).
According to the Financial Times report, renters spend only 55 percent of the amount spent by homeowners on gardening.
The study had also revealed that more and more people live in highly urbanized dwellings without gardens. In addition, the size of the garden decreases as the front yard is increasingly paved as a parking space. These factors also threaten the horticultural area, the estimated value of which is about 5 billion pounds per year.
In London's metropolitan area, housing ownership has fallen from 61% to 43% in six years, where horticultural products spend the least per household in the whole country.
However, according to HTA and the British Garden Association (RHS), an increase in housing rentals or a reduction in the size of a garden does not mean that people cannot grow plants.
Guy Barter, RHS’s Chief Horticultural Adviser, says that ‘plants to be grown in portable containers, for example, are particularly popular among apartment renters, as such growing containers can be included in the move if necessary.’
According to HTA's report, 1997–2005/2005 was the heyday of horticultural resale due to an increase in the ownership and wealth of dwellings from the late 80's to the mid-90s.
The report also predicts that trade will increase this year due to better weather conditions, after last year's March and April weather was worse.
Turkey interrogates U.S. diplomat over espionage report
Turkey's Foreign Ministry has summoned the country's most experienced U.S. diplomat to provide a report on spying carried out by Americans and Britons in Turkey.
According to Deputy Prime Minister Bulent, the U.S. embassy's attorney and Turkish authorities discussed the report on Monday.
Der Spiegel magazine and The Intercept have written that documents leaked by Edward Snowden, a former Analyst of the U.S. National Security Agency (NSA), revealed that Turkey was the main spy target of the U.S. and British intelligence services.
According to Turkish news agencies, Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan downplayed the significance of the report, saying that all the major states have spied on each other.
Earlier report on spying by Germany's main intelligence service on Ankara sparked a more angry reaction in the Turkish government.
