Magaluf police chief in Mallorca accused of corruption
Those who made the case presented evidence of extortion by police and Calvià city government officials at the Mallorcan Public Prosecutor's Office on Friday.
Calvià's police chief, located on Mallorca's holiday island, has been detained as a result of corruption allegations made by shops and bartenders in the Magaluf region.
Superintendent José Antonio Navarro was placed in pre-trial detention after several business owners on Punta Ballena filed corruption charges against him. Most of Magaluf's bars and nightclubs are located on Punta Ballena Street.
According to Mallorca Diario, which was published online, those who made the case revealed evidence of extortion by police and Calvià city government officials at the Office of the Prosecutor for Corruption in Mallorca on Friday.
The Spanish National Guard arrested two other local police officers in connection with corruption charges, and they will be questioned by a judge together with Navarro.
The daily newspaper ABC, published throughout the country, wrote that nightclub owners were angered by the bias of the authorities, which significantly complicated their business.
‘It’s no longer money, it’s survival,’ one business owner told the court.
You won't destroy our livelihood.
We've got nothing to lose.
Magaluf made headlines in several countries this summer when a YouTube video circulated online, in which an 18-year-old British vacationer had oral sex with 24 men during a so-called pub crawl.
Since then, the island's authorities have been trying to curb the intoxicated and uncontrollable behaviour of Magaluf's holidaymakers by reducing the number of participants in notorious, alcohol-swept pub crawls.
In addition, the Playhouse nightclub, where the oral sex video was filmed, was ordered to close for a year, and Carnage, the owner of the nightclub and the organizer of the pub crawl, was ordered to pay a joint fine of 55 000 euros.
Above all, the so-called balconing trend has also become popular among young British tourists in the popular tourist destination Magalufi, which has caused a number of accidents due to alcohol consumption, when people have jumped from one balcony to another or from the balcony to the hotel's swimming pool.
Israeli children return to school after the Gaza war
Thousands of Israeli children in the Gaza Strip returned to school on Monday after spending the summer in bomb shelters when rockets and mortars bombed their communities during the 50-day Israel-Hamas War and the schools remained closed as the area recovered from the fighting.
The opening of the schools caused joy and excitement in the communities bombed by rockets in the communities of southern Israel, but traces of the battles can still be seen.
In Pashosh kindergarten in Ashdod in the south, workers removed fragments from the walls and sliding before the children's arrival when the rocket struck the kindergarten.
“We’re a little scared, but we’re also thrilled,” says Ronit Bart, who lives on a kibbutz called Saad in the Israeli commune and works as an English teacher at the city’s school.
Many children living in this area desperately need routines.
Bart’s 11-year-old daughter Shani Bart thinks going back to school after a long time feels “a little strange.”
‘We’ve had a hard time when we didn’t leave our homes,’ he says.
President Reuven Rivlin visited a kibbutz near the Gaza border to provide his support.
Before last week's ceasefire, thousands of residents of communities in the Saad border region had stayed indoors or left their homes for safer areas further away from Gaza to escape the fire of rockets and grenades.
Many residents of the Nahal Oz community in the vicinity of the Gaza Front are skeptical of a comeback after a Palestinian grenade was killed by a 4-year-old boy.
According to the Ministry of Education, more than ten families had not yet returned.
For the time being, the children of these families have been placed in alternative schools.
Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu visited a school in Sderot, a town near the Gaza border that was the subject of heavy Palestinian fire.
He encouraged the children to study diligently and promised that “children’s education and safety will be ensured”.
Israel and Hamas agreed on a ceasefire until further notice last Tuesday.
The ceasefire immediately interrupted the fighting, but it did not provide a solution to many key issues, such as Hamas's demand for the removal of the Israeli-Egyptian blockade and the reopening of Gaza's aerial and seaways.
Israel wants to disarm Hamas and restore the bodies of two Israeli soldiers killed in the war.
Egypt is expected to start a new round of indirect talks later this month.
According to estimates by Palestine and the United Nations, more than 2,100 Palestinians were killed in the war, of whom three-quarters were civilians and at least 494 children.
According to Israel, at least half of those killed were soldiers, although there was no solid evidence of this.
On the Israeli side, 66 soldiers and six civilians were killed, including a Thai worker.
Hamas and other Gaza soldiers fired 4,591 rockets and grenades into Israeli cities in battle, mostly in the south.
The Israeli army, on the other hand, carried out more than 5 000 airstrikes and other attacks.
Thousands of homes were damaged or destroyed by Israeli strikes in Gaza, and an estimated 250 000 people escaped shelter from more than 100 U.N.-run schools that had been transformed into temporary shelters.
Tens of thousands of people were still sheltered and, as the battles continued, school authorities postponed the start of school the week before.
‘I hope that the school will open soon, and we can go back to school, just like other children in the world, and just like Jewish children,’ said 13-year-old Mohammad Amara, who lives in a school sheltered in the city of Gaza.
According to a commonly used meter, house prices have risen most to seven years in Australia's winter months.
RP Data CoreLogic Hedonic, which measures housing prices in the Australian capital, rost by 1.1 percent in August, according to an RP data on Monday.
With the rise during June, July and August, the total increase in prices was 4.2 per cent, which is more than in the same months after 2007.
The annual price increase was 10.9 per cent, which is more than twice as high as in the 12 months prior to August 2013, but prices have not risen steadily across the country.
According to Tim Lawless, RP Data's research director, Sydney and Melbourne are at the top of price increases.
RP Data's results show that house prices rose by 16.1 per cent in Sydney and 11.7 per century in Melbourne the year before.
The housing market in Adelaide, Brisbane and Darwin is the next busiest, with an average price rise of five to six per cent.
The weakest housing market is in Canberra, where prices rose by only 1.4 per cent per year as a result of the government's spending cuts.
According to Lawless, the spring that began in Australia will increase the number of apartments for sale in the coming months, which is a real test for the housing market.
“Given the increase in the sales of apartments to be auctioned, the overall high pace of sales and the continuing low interest rate, it is likely that house prices will continue to rise over the next three months,” Lawless said.
The new Gösta Museum has attracted record-breaking audiences to Mänttä
The new museum and visual arts have attracted record-breaking audiences to Mänttä this summer.
More than 70 000 visitors have visited the Serlachius Art Museum by the end of August.
The new Gösta Museum was opened in June, displaying, among other things, the classic works of pop art from Andy Warhol and Roy Lichtenstein.
