Kenia draws up a registration of civil servants to remove ‘ghost workers’
Kenya has begunned to draw up a biometric register of civil servants while trying to remove “ghost workers” from the government’s payroll.
Workers who did not enroll into the register during the next two weeks would no longer receive a salary.
The government suspects that thousands will continue to be paid when they have left their official duties.
President Uhuru Kenyatta pledged to eradicate corruption in the public sector after taking office in 2013.
According to an audit carried out earlier this year, “ghost workers’ salaries and other financial misconveniences amounted to at least one million dollars per month.
The government suspects that salaries will be paid into the account even after a person has died or left his official duties, the BBC's Wanyama Chebusiri reported from the capital Nairobi.
All officials must report themselves to the register in identification centers over the next few weeks in order to ensure that their data is stored in a biometric form of registration.
According to the press release, everyone who fails to do this without a proper reason will be removed from the payroll.
“This measure rationalizes the public sector significantly, as this determines how many people actually work. In addition, this clears payroll at both levels of the government. This solves the problem of ‘ghost workers’,” says Anne Waiguru, an official of the Ministry’s Cabinet responsible for decentralisation and planning.
