According to Berkeley, the housing market is “normal” again
One of London’s main developers has warned that the South-East England housing market has ‘returned’ to a normal level of activity.
London's housing has been plagued by huge demand and a sharp rise in prices while fearing a widespread credit bubble, prompting the Bank of England to impose restrictions on housing loans in June.
Tony Pidgley, the founder and chairman of Berkeley’s high-level developer, said on Monday that ‘since the beginning of the current financial year, the market and the number of deals have returned to normal since the 2013 spike’, adding that this has enabled a “stable operating environment.”
During the recession, the housing market in London went well during the recession, with foreign investors aspiring hordes into the capital.
According to data from the Land Registry, house prices in the city rose by 18.5 per cent in the previous year alone and thus clearly exceeded the English-Wales average of 6.7 per cent.
Berkeley's average sales prices for private, affordable and student buildings rose by about a fifth in the previous year, reaching an increase of 423 000 pounds by the end of April.
However, the strengthened pound has made the properties in London less attractive in the last few months - the interest of some has also been influenced by new property tax laws and political rhetoric about a possible “mansion tax” before the general elections in May.
Last week, Foxtons, a London-based real estate agent, warned that April's Mortgage Report reported on stricter lending regulations would lead to lower market growth in terms of both property sales volumes and prices in the second half of the year.
Recent data from the Bank of England on Monday showed that the number of approved loans declined in July, suggesting a cooling housing market.
According to its 2015 forecast of real estate prices, real estate agent Hamptons International has lowered prices by three per century because the index measuring homeowner's opinion on prices has already begun to decline.
The number of deals made in London's most expensive areas in Chelsea, Mayfair and Kensington has fallen by a quarter from a year ago, according to real estate agent WA Ellis.
However, the demand for homes in the capital is a blessing for Berkeley, which increased revenues from sold properties to more than 2.2 billion pounds.
Pidgley added that ‘the demand for a well-planned, ‘the right product in the best locations has remained stable, and therefore sales have remained lively.’
In June, the company announced that it had selled 3,742 new homes the year before April, which is almost one-third more than at the pre-eclinary peak in 2007.
The annual pre-tax profits rose by 40 per cent year-on-year, resulting in 380 million pounds and net sales increased by 18 per cent to 1.6 billion pounds.
Pidgley announced on Monday, prior to the company's annual meeting, that Berkeley's merits of the year would meet the market's current expectations.
Analysts agree that the pre-tax profit for the whole year is 450 million pounds.
Berkeley's share sold steadily for 23.96 pounds on the London Stock Exchange this afternoon.
