Lesotho's army denies planning strike; prime minister stays in South Africa
Lesotho's military officials have denied framing an attack on the plunge of the government, saying they were acting against a police officer suspected of trying to arm political fanatics.
Prime Minister Thomas Thabane fled the country, saying that the army had besieged his official residence and seized government buildings in Maseru.
The head of state traveled with his family to neighboring South Africa after saying he had received a threat of death.
Army spokeswoman Major Ntlele Ntoi denied the attack and said the army thus responded to a threat that came from “political fanatics.” The police are said to have attempted arming fanatics.
“Lesotho’s armed forces took action after obtaining a number of intelligence reports that some police forces are trying to arm young party fanatics when they are ready to cause problems,” he told the Voice of America news agency.
Clayson Monyela, Speaker of the South African government, said the army's actions looked like a revolutionary attempt.
“Although there has been no claim to have overturned the government by force, the actions of the Lesotho Armed Forces show the hallmarks of a revolutionary attempt in all respects,” he said.
According to Lesotho's military officials, the soldiers will return to their barracks on Sunday, with a peaceful atmosphere in the capital.
In the meantime, Deputy Prime Minister Mothetjoa Metsing will lead the government in Thabane's absence.
Thabane says he believes it's targeted because he's been trying to fight corruption.
Tensions have occurred in Lesotho since June, when Thabane halted parliamentary sessions due to disagreements between the Grand Coalition.
