Four tips for better underwater photos and videos
If you want to take pictures or a video underwater, you'll have several different device options to choose from.
The most economical option is a water-resistant, compact point-and-shoot camera such as the Nikon Coolpix AW120 or an action camera such as the GoPro Hero3+ Silver Edition, both of which cost about 300 dollars.
Personally, I took these pictures at the family party using several different cameras, each of which cost less than 350 dollars.
Regardless of the device, there are a few rules to follow.
Always check your device twice.
Even if you have a waterproof camera, make sure that the camera battery and other parts are waterproofly sealed.
In addition, set the camera's shooting settings to match the photos or video you want to take.
Some cameras or combinations of the camera and camcorder have a setting to optimize the shooting conditions underwater.
In addition – before jumping into the water, make sure how deep your device can be.
Some cameras can only withstand a depth of 5 feet, others up to a depth of 50 to 60 feet.
Check out both standard and waterproof models in our buying guide and digital camera reviews.
Take several pictures – many of them will fail in some way.
The compact cameras have an LCD display that allows you to compose an image, while action cameras often don't have this feature.
Even if you have an LCD, it is difficult to see it underwater, and the composition of the image can either succeed or fail.
So take several pictures.
If your camera also allows bracketing, which means taking multiple shots at once with different settings, take advantage of this feature.
Stay close to the surface.
The light decreases significantly deeper.
If possible, stay close to the surface when photographing in a swimming pool, lake or sea.
Here's how to get more colors for your photos; the deeper you go, the fewer colors you'll see.
Try to get close to the subject you're photographing.
This is an important piece of advice when shooting on land, but even more important underwater, because the light conditions are sparse.
It's very important when you're using an action camera: these devices often have a fixed lens with a wide angle of view, which means that the photographer needs to be close to the subject in order to fill the entire image up to the frames.
China won't let Hong Kong choose its leaders – protesters swore revenge
On Sunday, China's parliament ruled that Hong Kong's constituents would not be allowed to choose candidates for the 2017 elections, despite the growing unresolution that requires democratic reform.
The decision is likely to give a glimpse to the long-awaited protests in Hong Kong's business world block after activists begin planning and carrying out demonstrations just hours since the decision was released.
The decision of the Chinese People's Congress will effectively give the communists the power to eradicate all candidates who do not obey Beijing.
“This was to be expected, but it’s still infuriating,” said Emily Lau, chairman of the Democratic Party.
That's not what Beijing promised.
They've lied to the Hong Kongers.
It is clear that we are dealing with an autocratic regime.
Li Fei, the deputy secretary-general of the Chinese People’s Congress Committee, defended the Chinese government, saying that standing as a general nomination when electing Hong Kong’s leader would be too “chaotic.”
Since 1997, when Great Britain handed over Hong Kong to China, Beijing promised residents of the region that it would be allowed to elect its own leader from 2017 onwards.
According to China's leaders, Sunday's decision was a democratic breakthrough because it allowed Hong Kong to vote in direct elections, but it also makes it clear that China wants to strictly control the electoral process through the nomination committee, which is administered from Beijing.
According to the new clause, only candidates who “love the country and love Hong Kong” are accepted.
In the summer preceding the decision, some of the region's largest and most eye-catching protests took place in Hong Kong for years.
The campaign to defend democracy was largely driven by the Occupy Central With Love and Peace movement, the organizers of which have threatened to block the business quarter if Beijing did not grant Hong Hong Kong genuine universal suffrage.
On Sunday night, just hours after the decision was announced, hundreds of Occupy Central supporters gathered in the rain in front of Hong Kong's main government buildings.
At the demonstration, organizers of the movement announced that the movement would move into a new era of civil disobedience and they would bring about waves of protest in the coming weeks.
However, they did not explain the details, apparently fearing that it would lead to problems with the authorities.
Organizers said the movement ‘considers the Occupy Central stunt a last resort that will only be deployed if there is no hope of dialogue and there is no other way.’
We are very sorry to announce that today the last opportunity for dialogue has collapsed and the takeover of the city centre will take place.
Hong Kong authorities have been preparing for Beijing's announcement for days, and on Sunday security measures were tight near the government's main buildings, including police and barricades.
In Hong Kong, restlessness is fuelled by the feeling that residents are slowly losing power over their city.
A wave from the mainland has sparked competition for products and services.
In addition, there is a fear that Hong Kong's values, such as democracy and freedom of speech, are disappearing under the increasing pressure in Beijing.
Some have criticized the Occupy Central movement because its demonstrations threaten Hong Kong’s lifeblood – the business world.
“The protest they’re talking about can lead to major financial losses, depending on how many people take part in it and how long it lasts,” said Legislator Regina Ip, who’s been criticizing the movement for a long time.
We don't want to spread the worry that Hong Kong is slipping out of our grasp.
Such an image would be bad for investing.
Stories have also been presented in Chinese state media that Hong Kong’s democracy activists are henchmen of the Western powers that found the revolution.”
This summer, activists held an unofficial referendum on voting rights, which was attended by 780 000 people – more than a fifth of Hong Kong voters.
In July, tens of thousands took part in one of the region's largest pro-democratic demonstrations.
