Genetic abnormality is often misdiagnosed
For years, a British woman feared death due to a wrong diagnosis.
Karin Rodgers thought for most of her youth that she only had a few years left to live because she had been diagnosed with another disease.
In reality, he suffered from Charcot-Marie-Tooth's disease (CMT). It refers to a number of hereditary diseases that damage nerves outside the brain and spine.
According to Charity CMT UK, a wrong diagnosis is a common problem in CMT cases because so little is known about them.
It is estimated that around 23 000 people in the UK suffer from CMT, which causes symptoms in motor skills, such as muscle weakness, difficult walking and toe-to-threase.
The disease can also cause numbness or pain, and it is incurable and progressive, which means that the symptoms will worsen over time.
When Rodgers was 13 years old, he thought he had Friedreich's ataxia (FA), which has a very poor survival rate.
Rodgers thought he'd go into a wheelchair before turning 18 and die before reaching the age of 30.
Today, the 51-year-old mother of two says, “As a child, I knew I couldn’t do the same things as the others.
I fell every day and it took me longer than the others.
I could never roller skating or skateboarding with other kids, and I was bullied at school because I was walking and running in a different way than the others.
When Rodgers was 13 years old, he underwent several Achilles tendon surgeries to straighten his legs. In connection with the surgeries, he quickly glanced at his examination papers saying he was suffering from FA disease.
‘I felt guilty because I was so curious, so I didn’t tell anyone, but when I got home, I checked the information about the disease in the library and wrote to the FA Association,’ he says.
When I received information from them, I was shocked and in a panicky state.
I thought I'd go into a wheelchair by the age of 18 and die before I turned 25, and I'd slowly lose all my ability to function.
I went through this by myself and planned my own funeral.
Rodgers says that by the age of 17, he stated that his walk had not deteriorated as much as he had thought and asked his doctor about this.
The doctor just hugged me and said, ‘God, I don’t think you’ve got this disease, otherwise you’d be in a wheelchair.’
I think you're suffering from something significantly less dangerous.
According to some genetic tests, he was diagnosed with CMT disease.
‘When I was explained what CMT disease was, I thought I’d been postponed,’ he said.
Charity CMT UK organizes the CMT month to draw attention to the disease.
