The decline in the department store trade also continued in July, according to Grocery Trade Association.
According to it, the turnover of department store and hypermarket chains was 3.2 per cent lower in July than one year earlier.
The sales of home and leisure products have been the worst, with sales falling by more than seven per cent year-on-year.
The clothing products also sold clearly weaker than in the previous summer.
By contrast, the value of grocery retail sales grew by almost three per cent year-on-year in July.
Sales grew the most in small shops and large supermarkets.
During the first half of the year, i.e. January-July, the turnover of department stores and hypermarkets has contracted by more than two per cent.
On the other hand, sales of groceries have grown by almost one per cent.
