Confirmation on Bottas plot - almost 1.5 acres from the shore of the lake
Nastola rewards formula driver Valtteri Bottas on the beachfront plot.
The municipal government unanimously approved the handover of a plot of 1.4 hectares at its meeting on Monday.
In the future, a holiday home will be built on the property.
Nastola has been monitoring Bottas' success since his go-kart times.
He is perceived as a good ambassador to market Lahti, the Lahti region and Nastola.
The gift plot is located in Koiskala, on the shores of Lake Kärkjärvi.
It has been owned by the municipality of Nastola as part of a larger real estate transaction.
There are seven demolition buildings in the area, of which the municipality will dismantle six at the expense by the end of this year.
Bottas won his fourth podium this year in Formula 1, Belgium.
He's fifth in the standings.
Bottas is a native of Nastola and has lived in Villähte during his childhood.
He currently lives in England, but visits Nastola every time you are free of formulas.
Bottas has visited secondary schools and youth facilities.
Design-smart clothes are coming - but hardly for the poor
It introduces and tests them at the ongoing U.S. Open tennis competition in New York.
Ralph Lauren's smart jerseys are tested by the field's ball characters, but amateur Marcus Giron, one of the university players' greats, also wears one.
According to him, the shirt will help him follow his own development and regulate his recovery.
There are silver sensors woven into the nylon shirt, from which the information is conveyed to the mokkula on the shirt and from there to a smartphone or tablet.
Sensors measure the heart rate, rhythm of breathing, stress and steps.
Before throwing the shirt into the washing machine, the mokkula, which acts as the central unit, is removed.
The fashion house hasn't given an exact time to start selling the shirt, and it didn't even hint at its price, the Tech Times reported.
Raph Lauren isn't the only company that invests in smart clothing.
Athos has launched a smart jersey and smart shorts.
They measure heart rate, breathing and muscle work.
The clothes cost a hundred dollars apiece and the central unit the size of their thumb costs two hundred dollars.
Red Orbit also wrote about techno clothes.
A smart way to save in textbooks
The cost of university books in America exceeds US$1,000 for many students, so Matt Schroeder, who is about to start his second year of study, came up with a smart way to save on costs.
He developed a system in which students of later years borrow their books for a nominal fee instead of selling the books right away.
‘My book of mathematics would normally have cost US$180; I paid for the entire semester 10 dollars, as well as a hefty sandwich, according to 19-year-old Schroeder, who is studying at Covenant College in the state of Georgia.
Schroeder says his last semester's books would normally have cost 430 dollars.
Instead, he spent 120 bucks on them.
According to the university's board of directors, the average student spends more than 1,200 dollars a year on textbooks and school supplies, but students have access to many means to reduce these costs.
Students increasingly end up using online stores and creative approaches such as Schroeder's idea.
An increasing number of students are renting textbooks, allowing them to use the book throughout the semester and pay significantly less than students who buy the book.
According to Neebo Inc, which operates more than 250 campus bookstores, renting textbooks has doubleded since 2011.
Statistical data on the field reveal that about a quarter of university bookstores' bookstores were rental books this spring, says Trevor Meyer, chairman of Neebo.
According to the National Association of College Stores, less than half of all books are sourced from campus bookstores.
Here's the simplest way to save money.
Online stores
In online stores, a new book can only be obtained for one-third of the price it would cost in a campus bookstore.
Ron Larson, Bruce Edwards and Robert Hostetler’s “Calculus” ninth-edition has a recommended price of almost 290 dollars, but the online store of the specialty books Chegg.com for 239.99 dollars.
Build used
Used books are a good option if the reader isn't disturbed by other people's notes and worn-out appearance.
For example, the ‘Calculus’ book can be accessed from Chegg.com for 93.49 dollars.
Matt Casaday, a last year student at Brigham Young University for 25 years, says he bought the book “Strategic Media Decisions: Understanding The Business End Of The Advertising Business” from Amazon.com for just 0.42 dollars.
The new book would have cost 48 dollars.
Ingrid Bracey, president of Amherst's College Without Walls at the University of Massachusetts, and other academics recommend that students ask their professors if previous editions can be used in the course.
Occasionally, the reforms of the new edition are not essential for teaching.
In this case, the old edition can only be found for a fraction of the price that should be paid for the new.
In addition to Chegg and Amazon bookstores, eBay Inc's Half.com is a popular place to buy used books.
Renting
You should consider renting textbooks if you take good care of them, remember to return them and do not need them after completing the course.
By renting, you can save more than 80 percent on the price of a new book.
For example, rentinging a ‘Calculus’ book for the whole semester costs about 20 dollars.
Take a look at the rental company's rules, such as notes, the use of the highlighter, and the fees you're wearing.
Renter, be on your guard: if you forget to return the book, the late fee may be higher than the purchase price of the book.
E-books
Acquiring e-books instead of ordinary papers may also be out of the question.
These books are sometimes rented, in which case the right to use an e-book is paid to its holder, such as Amazon.com, Chegg or Barnes & Noble Inc.
You can get an ‘Calculus’ e-book from the Chegg store for six months for about 61 dollars.
According to Bracey, many literary students save the most, because several classics today can be downloaded for free, while books in the field of science and technology can be very expensive.
Comparing different vendors is worthwhile, whichever option you choose.
Joe Gault, 29, who is about to start Pepperdinen in law, recommends taking advantage of the book ISBN to determine the price, as it allows you to make sure you're looking for the right book.
Before ordering online, make sure that the book is definitely in stock, Gault says.
He himself learned this the hard way.
The delivery of the book he bought was frozen for four weeks, and he eventually had to get the book at full price from the university's bookstore.
Legion Theatre in Tampere is 20 years old
Founded in 1994, the theatre opposes unemployment, social exclusion and passivity.
Everyone who wants to make performing arts is welcome to participate.
Organizes expression-driven recreational activities for long-term unemployed persons aged 17-35.
Presentations often take a stand on society.
In recent years, there have been, among other things, Depressive Depression Moderata, Misme! - humorous stories about narcissism and classic adaptations.
Believes that every person can change their own life.
Nearly twice the number of people who died in the fires last year
Nearly than twice the number of people killed in the fires this year compared to last year.
According to the Finnish National Rescue Association (Spek), so far 55 people have been killed in 49 different fires.
Last year there were 31 fire deaths.
Most of those who died, 36, have been men.
There have been 11 people over 65 years of age
According to the organisation's fire death statistics, the situation was last worse in 2009, when by the end of August, 64 people had died in fires.
Most fire deaths take place in apartments.
There have been 17 fire deaths in blocks of flats, 21 in detached houses and seven in terraced and other detached houses.
Spek reminds you to review your home's fire safety.
According to Spekki, at least all home smoke alarms should be ensured.
In addition, everyone should check that the initial extinguishing equipment is easy to deploy.
There must also be an accessible exit route for all premises in your home.
Children should also be told what electrical appliances they are allowed to use when they are at home alone.
NATO Summit: First Protests in Newport and Cardiff
Opponents of the NATO summit protested in Newport last weekend.
On Saturday, hundreds gathered in the center of the city of Newport for a march against NATO.
On Sunday, about 150 people gathered at Cardiff City Hall for a so-called counter-peak meeting.
Stephen Fairclough followed the events over the weekend and spoke to the demonstrators, who had arrived all the way from Belgium.
Young people were rescued by air from Australia's Blue Mountains
TWO teenage hikers were lifted to refuge after spending the night stalled in the Blue Mountains in Australia's New South Wales region.
A 16-year-old girl and an 18-year-old man went to Govetts Leap in Blackheath after noon.
The relatives were worried about young people who hadn't returned home and called the police at eight o'clock in the evening.
A search patrol consisting of local police officers and helpers was deployed in search of youths, and the duo were found around 23 o'clock near the Bridal Veil Falls.
The girl had injured her knee, and the man had fallen and hit her head.
Officers spent the night with the couple and they were lifted to safety on Monday morning.
Hikers were taken by ambulance to the Blue Mountains Hospital, and their condition was stable.
Ukrainian Soldiers Withdrew From Luhansk Airport
Ukrainian troops have withdrawn from Luhansk airport.
According to the Ukrainian army, the soldiers were given a retreat order.
According to Ukraine, paratroopers have clashed fiercely with Russian tanks in the field.
According to an army spokesman, the gunfire of the opposing party has been so precise that the shooter is likely to be Russian professional soldiers.
In the past day, seven Ukrainian soldiers have been killed.
The Ukrainian patrol ship, which received artillery hits in the Asovan Sea off the coast of South-Eastern Ukrainia, sank in the morning, according to the Border Patrol.
Eight marines have been rescued and twelve are still missing.
The Border Patrol had no certainty as to where the ship was fired from.
Separatists said on social media yesterday that they had fired from scratch.
According to Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov, Russia will not invade Ukraine.
Lavrov, however, insisted that Ukrainian troops must immediately retreat into positions from which they cannot fire on civilians.
According to the Foreign Minister, an imminent ceasefire should be brought unconditionally during the Minsk talks today between Russia, Ukraine and the OSCE.
Lavrov also pointed out that Russia will respond to possible new economic sanctions.
First of all, we will protect our own interests, our finances, the social sector, our businesses.
At the same time, we will also draw conclusions about the actions of our partners, Lavrov threatened.
Heavy rains flooded Malmö and Copenhagen
The streets were flooded on Sunday in Malmö and Copenhagen as heavy rains whipped southern Sweden and Denmark.
The floodwaters cut off several roads, disrupted traffic and buried cars.
Authorities estimated on Sunday that it might take time to leash the flood, even though it was predicted that the rains would cease on Monday.
The heavy rains surprised the Swedish authorities, and there was no weather warning in advance for the rains.
The Finnish Meteorological Institute's on-call meteorologist Ari Mustala estimates that the rain front that plagued the area was very local.
The precipitation area looks narrow and stationary.
It's no wondress if it's been hard to predict.
“French socialists should ‘be quiet and help France,” Francois Hollande’s chief supporter said
Valls urged the deeply divided left to ‘look at their feelings’ towards the troubled socialist president, whose composition of the new government did not gain the approval of the French majority.
The president deserves everyone's respect, he deserves our loyalty, our support.
“It’s our job to stand by him,” Valls said, earning the applause.
As a calming gesture, the prime minister demanded that the government leave peace despite France's controversial 35-hour working week, the new economy minister Emmanuel Macron's suggestion earlier this week that the rule should be relaxed.
On Saturday, Hollande asked the socialists to work together with the government.
However, Minister of Justice Christiane Taubira caused a disagreement by appearing at a meeting of Socialist MPs expressing differing opinions and criticizing the Socialist Party for allowing “France to give up hope for the future.”
Marine Le Pen, the leader of the far-right National Front, mocked the socialist's constant division and said she didn't think the new Walls government would last more than a few months.
Francois Hollande may have gone to the war, but Manuel Valls, whose duty it was to form a new government since the last one didn't last even during the summer.
‘And the new government won’t last until autumn or winter,’ Le Pen told reporters.
He reiterated his party's proposal to dissolve Parliament and considered it certain his party's election victory in early elections and he himself would be ready to lead the party.
France's ruling party suffered a crushing defeat in the March municipal elections, and the extremist right held the list-scorer in May's EU elections.
According to an opinion poll published on Sunday, 76 percent of French people believe the Socialist Party could split into a number of rival groups before the end of Hollande's term in 2017.
Pascal Perrineau, a political scientist at Po University, warned that the French will soon lose patience unless the new socialist government manages to improve the economy and record-high unemployment.
‘There is a small chance of success, but the audience must quickly notice that the change is taking place,’ he warned.
Otherwise, the situation may get even worse.
Pokka betrayed Good Morning Finland's presenters Kirsi Alm-Siira, Nina Rahkola and meteorologist Pekka Pouda cheated badly in the recorded part of the programme.
