Alm-Siira goes crazy in her words when she tries to say the word budget session, and her freaking out also makes Pouda and Rahkola lose their money time and time again.
And these heroes should be allowed to run the live broadcast even tomorrow, the Good Morning Finland Facebook update says.
The video has become very popular on Facebook and it has been liked and shared on Facebook more than 10 000 times.
Jorma K. Pouta, 68, who worked at Lehtikuva's photo agency, finished third in the portrait category in 1972.
Israeli Prime Minister Golda Meir is pictured.
A meeting of the Socialist International was held at the Rakennusmestarit building on Fredrikinkatu in Helsinki in May 1971.
There were a lot of heads of state at the meeting and the security measures were enormous.
When Golda Meir went to the bathroom, seven bodyguards went along.
However, at the beginning of the meeting, we had more than five minutes to shoot.
I took this picture four to five feet away.
The light and the background were right.
Immediately I realized that this time I succeeded.
Meiri's expression shows that the leaders of the Middle East have had a hard time before.
That's when you did well in the competition with pictures of well-known individuals or events.
It's great that you can thrive with other pictures nowadays, as long as there's an idea and visual wrangling.
It's the second best picture I've ever taken.
Best is Lasse Virén's fall from the Munich Olympics.
I didn't send it to the competition.
Dissertation: Aarnio Investigation has already cost more than five million euros
Investigating the legality of the activities of the Helsinki Police Department has already cost more than five million euros and demanded 30–50 person-years.
This is the assessment of Jyri Rajamäki in his doctoral thesis for the University of Jyväskylä.
Rajamäki proposes that supervising the legality of the activities of the police and the prevention of criminal offences in general could be significantly improved by combining IT systems for investigations and legal proceedings.
According to Rajamäki, modern technology enables the multi-use of forensic material.
He states that it is possible to build a system in which the information collected in order to investigate a crime is automatically electronic evidence that fills the evidence chain and at the same time enables comprehensive monitoring of legality.
The dissertation will be inspected on Saturday.
YLE: The 1999 idea of vegetarian food was forgotten on the wall of the school: "Fish and chicken dishes belonging to the Vegetarian Diet"
A picture of a special bulletin on the wall of a women's trip school in Pirkkala caused a food controversy on social media, Yle says on its website.
Vegetarians!
Vegetarian diet at school lunch includes fish and chicken dishes.
Only when there's meat on the list, we'll make a vegetarian option!
This is stated in a 15-year-old bulletin that was forgotten on the school wall.
You can see the picture here.
The image that has spread on social media has prompted people to send indignant feedback to the municipality of Pirkkala.
Arja Vaarma, the head of the food and cleanliness service, tells YLE that the note that was forgotten on the wall is not true and should have been removed a long time ago.
Apparently, before my arrival, there has sometimes been such an idea that was discussed with health care at the time.
Every student receives a meal according to their wishes, he says to Yle.
On this day, Pirkkala's schools offer pupils a vegetarian food option only with the permission of their parents and a doctor's or public health nurse's certificate of a vegetarian diet.
Vaarma justifies the solution for Yle by the fact that vegetarian food experiments among young people may leave their nutrition incomplete, and the absence of meat or chicken may arouse wonder from the pupils' parents.
Norwegian Cruise close to US$3 billion Prestige Cruise
Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings Ltd NCHL.O, the world's third-largest cruise company, negotiates to buy its rival Prestige Cruises International Inc for three billion dollars. The information comes from people who are closely involved.
As a result of the transaction, the Norwegian Cruise company, which has a market value of 6.8 billion dollars, would have access to Prestige Cruises luxury cruisers and wealthy customers as it continues to compete against the larger Royal Caribbean Cruises Ltd (RCL.N) and Carnival Corp (CCL.N).
According to the sources, information about a possible agreement may be published already this week, although it is also possible that the negotiations will not bring results.
Prestige Cruises' owner, the private equity firm Apollo Global Management LLC (APO.N), also owns 20 percent of Norwegian Cruise.
Sources didn't want their names to be made public, because the negotiations aren't held in public.
Representatives of Norwegian Cruise and Prestige Cruises did not respond to requests for comment and Apollo's spokesman declined to comment.
The Miami-based Norwegian Cruise company has 13 cruise ships with a route map covering North America, the Mediterranean, the Baltic Sea, Central America and the Caribbean.
Its revenue grew by 13 percent from 2012 to 2013, when its turnover was US$2.57 billion.
Prestige Cruises, which also operates from Miami, sails under the Oceania and Regent brand names, which include a total of eight cruise ships, and the route map covers Scandinavia, Russia, the Mediterranean, North America, Asia, Africa and South America.
Its turnover in 2013 was US$1.2 billion, which is six per cent more than in the year before.
The 29-billion-dollar cruise industry is expected to benefit from the rise of emerging economies such as China and India in the coming years.
The companies are competing to become the number one option for these new customers as they choose a cruise.
Prestige Cruises registered its first stock issue on the U.S. stock market in January 2014.
Apollo has owned most of the company's shares since the 2007 acquisition, which was worth US$850 million.
Norwegian Cruise was founded in 2000 with Genting Bhd (GENT.KL), the entertainment and casino company of Malaysian billionaire Lim Kok Thay.
Apollo invested one billion dollars at Norwegian Cruise in 2008.
Norwegian Cruise was listed as a public company in January 2013.
According to published data, Genting accounted for 28 per cent of the company, Apollon 20 per cent and private equity firm TPG Capital LP accounted for 8 per cent from the end of June onwards.
Carnival, Royal Caribbean Cruises and Norwegian Cruise companies together account for 82 per cent of North American cruise capacity, according to a registration document published by Prestige Cruise in connection with the issuance.
The Tampere City Board is discussing the matter today.
Animalia hopes that the City Board will provide strong support for the initiative.
According to Salla Tuomivaara, Animalia's Executive Director, who is preparing her doctoral dissertation for the University of Tampere, it is important to end briefly during dolphinaries in Finland and around the world.
In recent years, fortunately, there has been a growing view that the holding of advanced mammals such as dolphins in dolphinaries is neither animal-protent nor ethically sustainable.
We would also like this view to come to Tampere.
The abolition of the dolphinarium is not only an animal rights issue, but also a question about Tampere's image as a tourist destination, Tuomivaara points out.
According to Tuomivaara, the death of the Näsi dolphin strengthens the requirements for the shutdown of dolphinary operations.
Näsi's death is a sad continuation of the dark history of the Särkänniemi dolphinarium.
It's time to put a dolphin's point, close the dolphin jail and start figuring out how dolphins in Tampere could be moved to a demarcated sea area, a kind of dolphin shelter," Tuomivaara says.
Näsi did not have time to return to the dolphins' original habitat, but hopefully the rest dolphins living in Särkänniemi will have time to experience even more life than a stimulated concrete basin, Tuomivaaraka hopes.
Animal Welfare Federation Animalia's goal is that the use of all wild animal species in circuses would be prohibited in the comprehensive reform of the Animal Welfare Act under preparation.
Dolphinariums are also included.
In support of the requirement, Animalia's Executive Director Tuomivaara participates in a demonstration on behalf of dolphins, which will be held today 1.9.
A demonstration at the Embassy of Japan in Helsinki will take a stand against the dolphin hunting season that will start in Japan.
During the hunting season, about 2300 dolphins are brutally captured or killed each year, either for dolphinariums or for food.
Putin urges Kiev to open talks on a new state with eastern Ukraine
On Sunday, Russian President Vladimir Putin demanded that the Ukrainian government stop fighting separatists in the east of the country and immediately begin negotiations on the ‘statisticism’ of the resigning region, Russian news sources said.
His spokesman, Dmitry Peskov, later clarified that Putin did not mean that Separatist-ruled territory of Eastern Ukraine would become part of Russia, but that its status within Ukraine must be reconsidered in order to give the Russian-speaking population the power to defend one's own rights and interests.
Putin's Kiev's demand for talks with pro-Russian rebels as equal bargaining partners corresponds to his apparent strategy, which he has followed ever since the violence began five years ago: he seeks to help separatists capture the region and thus force the Ukrainian government to practically grant the newly proclaimed Novorossija independence, which allows it to go to Russia instead of the West.
In an interview with the State Channel 1, Putin condemned the Ukrainian army's efforts to retake the separatist-dominated territory of eastern Donetsk and Luhansk, which separatists captured in March and April after Moscow annexed Ukraine's Crimean Peninsula on March 18.
The Kremlin and separatists have recently baptized the area as ‘Novorossiya, ‘New Russia’ – this term is reminiscent of the glory days that preceded the revolutions of the Russian superpower.
According to the Russian news agency Itar-Tass, Putin said the planned peace talks are an “illusion” for as long as Ukraine’s politicians are conducting campaigns for parliamentary elections to be held on 26 October, and government forces are attacking civilians in separatist-ruled areas.
‘We must immediately begin extensive negotiations, not only on technical issues, but also on the organisation of society and the status of the state of South-Eastern Ukrainia, taking into account the interests of the people who live there,” he said.
According to Peskov, Putin’s reference to ‘stateism’ relates to broader independence, which has been debated for months with Kiev’s leadership as it tries to dispel the fears of Russian-speaking territories that their cultural and linguistic rights would be threatened.
Only the Ukrainian government can allocate the independence they need for the eastern regions, Peskov says.
Discussions on this issue cannot take place between Ukraine and Russia, Peskov says, “because this is not a conflict between Russia and Ukraine, but an internal conflict between Ukraine.”
Kremlin's spokesperson's interlude aimed at correcting Putin’s “false interpretation”, emphasized that the Russian regime deals with the revolt of the separatists in the eastern regions in a different way than in the case of Crimea, when it virtually captured an area with a population of 2 million people ethnically Russian.
It would be considerably more difficult for Moscow to annex the Donetsk or Luhansk regions in eastern Ukraine, because the majority of the 6.5 million people living in the area are not ethnically Russian, and because, according to pre-conflict opinion polls, the majority of the population was in favour of staying in Ukraine.
Independence, discussed by Russian diplomats in international fora, would give Ukrainian local regimes the right to agree on their own trade agreements and external relations, which would give the Kremlin virtually power in an area connecting Russia to Crimea.
The Black Sea Peninsula, which was annexed to Russia five months ago, is home to Russia's main fleet, as well as commercial shipping port areas and historical coastal sites.
There are also mines, factories and metal digestories in the area between Rostov and Crimea in Russia, which produce products vital to the Russian army.
In his inauguration speech on June 7, Ukrainian President Petro Poroshenko proposed that the Ukrainian legislators – after the new elections – consider constitutional amendments in order to increase decision-making power in areas deviating from Ukraine’s core territory on their own economy and the status of languages.
However, his vision of independence seems to differ sharply from the views of the Kremlin and the separatists perhaps armed and incited by Moscow.
Putin made his latest call to the Kiev government for talks with pro-Russian rebels as equal partners after the rebels advanced further last week after Russian tanks and troops invaded eastern Ukraine from a previously peaceful region off the coast of the Azov Sea.
Ukraine's security authorities fear that the takeover by Russian-backed separatist the city of Novoazovsky is the beginning of a larger effort to capture the entire strategically significant coastal region all the way to Crimea.
It has led to a massive reinforcement of the city of Mariupol by civilians and military personnel. Mariupol is known as the steel-producing port city, located between Novoazovsk and the niche land leading to the Crimean Peninsula.
Poroshenko spoke to the leaders of the European Union in Brussels on Saturday, calling for further measures to prevent future violations of Russia in Ukraine, the former Republic of the Soviet Union, which has been independent for 23 years.
‘We’re about to reach a point where there’s no turning back,” Poroshenko warned.
Thousands of foreign soldiers and tanks are now present on Ukrainian territory.
The EU summit did not make any decisions on direct action; the leaders proposed further sanctions so that they could be introduced if the crisis in Ukraine flares again in unclear circumstances.
Ukraine's soldiers had to withdrew from their positions in the Ilovaisk region after two Russian armored detachments and 1 000 soldiers moved to the Donetsk region last week to strengthen troubled separatists, General Andriy Lysenko, a spokeswoman for Ukraine's National Security and Defense Council, told reporters on Saturday in Kiev.
According to Lysenko, the first of Russia's 63 Ukrainian soldiers in Ilovaiski was replaced by 10 Russian parachutists who were imprisoned on Ukrainian territory a week ago.
Milan's opening goal was scored by Japanese forward Keisuke Honda in the seventh minute of play.
In the second half Sulley Muntari was scored first by Sulley Muntari and a little later by Jeremy Menez from the penalty spot.
Lazio also got a penalty in the closing minutes of the game, but goaltender Diego Lopez blocked Antonio Candreva's shot.
Lopez moved to Milan this season from Real Madrid.
Milan's former striker icon Inzagh's coaching debut, San Siro's stadium was only half full.
